    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d193">

                <pb facs="0416" n="102" xml:id="pg102"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/23</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-09-12T09:00:00+01:00">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">September 12, 1914, 9 a.m.</hi>
                    </date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 10:30 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>110. I presented the matter referred to in the conversation between Mr. Oscar
                    Straus and Count Bernstorff in an informal manner not wishing to receive a
                    refusal so formal as to prevent future similar offers. Have had no answer but
                    know that time is not yet ripe. I think possibly the Kaiser’s letter to the
                    President about dumdum bullets, etc.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d193-f1"/>, offers an
                    opportunity to get in the situation in a conciliatory capacity and suggest that
                    you request me to investigate the charges made.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
